# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-sbcl-windows-build-missing-exe-suffix — SAVE-LISP-AND-DIE :EXECUTABLE T does not append .exe on the Windows runner
#+STATUS: OPEN
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: claude/ci-script-powershell-sf2s1g

* Context

build:alfe:windows (job 16000852460, and reproduced on MR !111) died on:

    autolisp-front-end/tools/alfe/bin/alfe-sbcl.exe : Le terme
    ...alfe-sbcl.exe... n'est pas reconnu comme nom d'applet de
    commande, fonction, fichier de script ou programme executable.

That is PowerShell's CommandNotFoundException. Two rounds of "fix the
PowerShell invocation" (adding the `&' call operator, then switching to a
fully-qualified absolute path via $env:CI_PROJECT_DIR) were each pushed to
MR !111 and each reproduced the EXACT SAME error -- which is the tell:
CommandNotFoundException on a fully-qualified absolute path cannot be a
PowerShell syntax/quoting issue, because a qualified path bypasses
PowerShell's command-name search entirely and goes straight to a file
existence check. The file was genuinely not there.

Added a diagnostic `Get-ChildItem` of tools/alfe/bin/ right after
`make -C autolisp-front-end build-alfe-sbcl` to confirm. It showed:

    Mode          LastWriteTime   Length Name
    ----          -------------   ------ ----
    -a---- 20/08/2026     10:44 56237024 alfe-sbcl

56 MB, the right ballpark for an SBCL core -- the build itself is fine.
The file is simply named `alfe-sbcl', with NO `.exe' suffix.

* Problem

tools/alfe/generate-alfe.lisp (and the shared generator it delegates to,
clautolisp/autolisp-reader/tools/read-autolisp/generate.lisp, used
identically by read-autolisp and clautolisp) calls:

    (apply #'sb-ext:save-lisp-and-die
           (namestring (merge-pathnames program-name release-directory nil))
           :executable t ...)

with PROGRAM-NAME = "alfe-sbcl" -- no extension. The assumption, visible
in the .gitlab-ci.yml comment this ticket's fix removes ("on Windows the
build now produces alfe-sbcl.exe (pjb, 2026-08-16)"), was that SBCL's
SAVE-LISP-AND-DIE appends .exe itself on Windows when :EXECUTABLE T is
given a name that lacks it. Measured on the real Windows CI runner, this
SBCL does NOT do that: it writes exactly the namestring it is given.

Since generate.lisp is shared, this affects read-autolisp and clautolisp
on Windows identically, not just alfe -- it happened to surface here
first because build:alfe:windows is the only Windows CI job that names
the .exe suffix explicitly and checks it.

* Fix

Added EXECUTABLE-PROGRAM-NAME to generate.lisp: on
(uiop:os-windows-p), append ".exe" to PROGRAM-NAME unless it is already
present, before merging with RELEASE-DIRECTORY and handing the result to
SAVE-LISP-AND-DIE / SAVE-APPLICATION. Do not rely on the implementation
to add the suffix; ask for it explicitly, since that is exactly the
assumption that silently failed here.

* Acceptance criteria

- build:alfe:windows produces tools/alfe/bin/alfe-sbcl.exe (confirmed by
  the Get-ChildItem sanity check kept permanently in that job) and the
  `alfe-sbcl.exe --version' probe succeeds.
- No regression to the Linux/macOS builds, which do not go through the
  Windows branch of EXECUTABLE-PROGRAM-NAME.

* Note

Filed while iterating on MR !111 (clautolisp/ogamita, gitlab.com) --
two prior CI-script-only fixes were pushed to that MR and each was
falsified by the pipeline itself before this root cause was found and
fixed at the source instead of patched around in CI.
